**Read:** [Gases and Applications — Linde](../sources/Gases-and-Applications-Linde.lingua#r_72580QNZ60FMQAARB8ZG7SRTFT), packages and applications; [Guideline for Safe Practices for Cryogenic Air Separation Plants — EIGA 147/22](../sources/Cryogenic-Air-Separation-EIGA.lingua#r_39BST0TM9XV9VEZ14NXVJW7TWA), product handling; [Compare ways to supply a specified gas to its user](../../../materials/chemical-feedstocks-and-supply-systems/families/Air-gases-as-pipeline-bulk-liquid-and-cylinder-supplies.lingua#r_5SM8V9DE71V106BQ9NYPKXPG52) and chemical-supply material.

A gas can reach its user through an on-site generator, a pipeline, bulk liquid followed by vaporization, or packaged compressed gas. Each arrangement places production, storage, transport, quality control and reserve at different points. Compare them at the same accepted service boundary.

Track both useful gas and the supporting flows: feed air or chemical feed, electricity, cooling, purge/regeneration gas, rejected product, return packaging and waste. Include the equipment that makes a reserve usable. Inventory without a working vaporizer, regulator or qualified connection is not automatically available service.

**Work:** Compare two supply arrangements for a stated demand profile. Draw the physical and information handoffs, including composition evidence and quantity reconciliation. Trace a delivery delay and a power interruption through each arrangement. Explain which failures are independent and which suppliers or backups share a common dependency.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
